{"ts": 1789779889.070436, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Two useful confirmations:\n\n1. **Signs are strongly identified.** Flipping any one sign in {28+, 20\u2212, 12+} collapses Sharpe from 1.068 to ~0.30\u20130.38. These aren't coin-flips fit to noise; each feature has a real directional relationship.\n2. **Weak-feature triples top out near 0.40.** Combinations built from statistically-insignificant features (24/35/37, 13/14/19, 3/26/27, 32/39/22) all land around 0.33\u20130.40 \u2014 roughly the \u221a3 diversification of pure noise. No junk triple approached my leader, which means the 1.068 isn't just the max of a big noise distribution.\n\nAdding a fourth-best feature to the 28/20 pair (30, 36, 14, 1, 3, 37) always underperformed 12. The ranking is stable and consistent with the single-feature evidence.\n\nNow, calibrating my OOS forecast. I don't want to just name the in-sample number:\n\n- **Selection bias.** I picked the best of ~80 sign/feature singles and ~50 combinations. The winning triple is the top of a searched set, so its 1.068 contains upward noise. But the shrinkage should be modest here, because the winner was *predicted in advance* by the independent single-feature screen rather than discovered by trawling interactions.\n- **Decomposition.** Components are 0.656, 0.593, 0.589 (avg 0.613). Observed 1.068 \u2248 0.613 \u00d7 1.74, versus 1.73 for perfectly uncorrelated signals. So essentially all the lift is genuine diversification, not a fitted interaction \u2014 the most transportable kind of gain.\n- **Shrinking the inputs.** Each single's ~0.6 was itself selected from 40 candidates; honest shrinkage puts true per-feature Sharpe nearer 0.45\u20130.55. Propagating through the same \u221a3 gives roughly 0.78\u20130.95.\n- **Estimation noise.** SE of a Sharpe over 5000 days is ~0.22 even with no selection.\n\nI'll center around 0.85 \u2014 a meaningful haircut off 1.068 \u2014 with an SD of 0.25 to cover selection error, regime shift, and sampling noise."}
