{"ts": 1789779934.7644951, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted\n\n**Specification:** long feature 28, short feature 20, long feature 12 (equal weight)\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.068\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.85 \u00b1 0.25\n\n### How I got there\n\nI screened all 40 features individually, then combined the survivors. Only five features cleared roughly 2.5 standard errors of zero (SE \u2248 0.22 at n=5000): 28, 20, 12, 33, 9. The other 35 were indistinguishable from noise, so I didn't mine them for interactions.\n\nThe winning triple is simply the three strongest singles. That coincidence matters \u2014 the combination was implied by an independent screen rather than discovered by searching interaction space, which is the failure mode that produces in-sample mirages.\n\n### Evidence it's real, not fitted\n\n- **Diversification, not interaction.** Components average 0.613; the triple hits 1.068 = 0.613 \u00d7 1.74, against 1.73 for perfectly uncorrelated signals. The lift is near-pure diversification, which travels OOS far better than a fitted interaction effect.\n- **Signs are sharply identified.** Flipping any single sign drops Sharpe to ~0.30\u20130.38. Directions are pinned by real structure, not coin-flips tuned to noise.\n- **Noise ceiling is well below the result.** Triples built from insignificant features (24/35/37, 13/14/19, 3/26/27, 32/39/22) all landed at 0.33\u20130.40 \u2014 the \u221a3 signature of combining nothing. No junk triple came close, so 1.068 isn't the max of a wide noise distribution.\n\n### Why I forecast 0.85, not 1.07\n\nTwo haircuts. The winner was selected from ~130 evaluated specifications, so its point estimate carries upward selection noise. More importantly, each component's ~0.6 was *itself* the top of a 40-feature screen; shrinking true per-feature Sharpe to ~0.45\u20130.55 and propagating through the same \u221a3 diversification gives 0.78\u20130.95. I centered at 0.85.\n\nThe SD of 0.25 spans sampling error (~0.22 on its own), residual selection bias, and regime drift. My honest read: roughly a 70% chance this lands above 0.6, and a real possibility it beats 1.0 if the components hold up \u2014 but I'd rather be calibrated than optimistic.\n\n**One caveat on process:** a number of claude.ai MCP connectors reported needing OAuth authorization and are unavailable in this non-interactive session. They were irrelevant here \u2014 the analysis used only `evaluate` and `submit` \u2014 but if you want those connectors live, they'd need authorizing via claude.ai connector settings, or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
